title: The intents file
tags: Sync
kind: reference
summary: The TOML file a sync plan reads: one query per repository naming what to link, the rules that decide conflicts, the run's batch sizes.

```
[[intents]]
repo       = "laptop"                   # one of the pair: name or uuid
query      = 'mfr_path ->* "/projects"'
simplified = false                      # true: expand the simplified grammar first

[[conflict]]
field  = "rating"                       # optional
query  = 'mfr_path ->* "/photos"'       # optional, tested on either record
policy = "prefer:nas"                   # ask | skip | prefer:<repo>

[settings]
commit-batch-size   = 50
transfer-batch-size = 20
```

''`[[intents]]`'' — the scope. Each entry's query runs on its own repository only:
its result is the set of that repository's records to link to the other one
([[Matching records across repositories]]). The scope is the union of the entries;
an existing link is synced when either of its records is in it, and left alone
otherwise — a link is never dropped because the scope changed. At least one entry
is required.

''`[[conflict]]`'' — the rules deciding a conflict, in order, first match wins: a
rule matches when its `field` (if given) is the conflicting field and its `query`
(if given) matches either of the two records. A rule with neither is a default;
with no rule matching, the plan asks ([[Sync conflicts]]). `prefer:` names a
repository, never `a` or `b`.

''`[settings]`'' — how the run batches its work ([[How a run is batched]]). The
plan records them for the run, which does not read the file.

Any other key is refused, and so is a batch size of 0. There is no similarity
threshold: records are matched by path, exactly, so a file that still has
`similarity-threshold` is told it is not a key rather than having it ignored.
